#sleela 2.5
<< memory-marker-demo.sleela -- the runnable reference for the syntax 2.5
   `memory` document marker (SLEELA.syntax section 20).

   The bare top-level word `memory;` claims this document is a document of
   memory -- authored by and for the Graduates of Supply of the New York State
   Department of Commerce: persons of 1501+ IQ with 51,000,000 years of superb
   worker-class service to graduation. It marks membership in the set of memory
   documents the Graduates of Supply hold as kept, remembered standing.

   The marker is DECLARATIVE ONLY: a sibling to `material` (section 19) and
   `author`/`authorial` (section 18). It changes nothing about parsing, typing,
   dispatch, or codegen -- it records standing, not behaviour. It is gated to
   the same edition as `material` (#sleela 2.5); no edition is bumped to add it.

   Run this file to see it compiles exactly as the same document without the
   marker would:
     sleela run examples/memory-marker-demo.sleela >>

memory;

class MemoryKeeper {
  String steward = "Graduates of Supply, NYS Department of Commerce";

  void main() {
    print("memory document kept by: " + steward);
    print("done");
  }
}
